window.HB_SEARCH = [{"t":"Composition: the basics","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html","x":"Twelve ways to arrange what’s in your frame, and why each one works. Composition is the order you bring to a scene. It’s about what you let into the frame and where you put it. Get it right and the viewer’s eye goes straight to what matters, then finds somewhere pleasant to rest. Get it wrong and the eye wanders around, never quite sure what the picture is about. None of these techniques is a law. They’re patterns that photographers and painters have noticed again and again, because they match the way our eyes move through a picture. Learn them well enough to spot them in the viewfinder without thinking. Then break them on purpose when a picture asks for it. Each one comes with its illustration from the cheat sheet, and the red lines show the idea at work. Take them one at a time. Read a technique, then go for a walk looking for that one thing, camera ready. Soon you’ll see lines and frames everywhere. Before long you’ll be combining them without trying. Each technique links to its exercise at the end of the chapter."},{"t":"Rule of thirds","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#rule-of-thirds","x":"A lone tree on the right third line, with the horizon on the lower third line. Picture two horizontal and two vertical lines cutting the frame into nine equal parts, like a noughts-and-crosses grid. Put the horizon on one of the horizontal lines instead of through the middle. Now the sky or the land gets the bigger share, and the picture looks like you meant it. Put your main subject where two lines cross. It gets weight without being stuck dead centre. Most cameras and phones can show this grid on screen, so turn it on. Use the lower line when the sky is the interesting part and the upper line when the foreground is. In a portrait, put the eyes near the upper line. It works just as well when you crop afterwards. If a picture feels static, try trimming one side so the subject moves onto a third. Many photographers shoot a little wider than they need for exactly this reason, then fine-tune the placement on the computer. Over time you’ll place things on the thirds without thinking, and that’s when it’s worth trying the centre again, on purpose."},{"t":"Leading lines","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#leading-lines","x":"A road and a line of telephone poles converging on a small house. Our eyes follow lines. Roads, railway tracks, rivers, fences and the edges of buildings all work as paths into a picture. When one starts near the bottom or a corner and runs towards your subject, the viewer travels along it and arrives right where you want them. Lines that meet at a vanishing point add depth too. A flat scene suddenly feels like a place you could walk into. Look for the line first and the subject second, then move until the line points the right way. Watch out for lines that run out of the frame. They take the viewer’s attention with them. Lines don’t have to be straight or obvious. A trail of footprints in sand will do the job, and so will a row of lamp posts or the edge of a shadow. Try crouching low with a wide lens so the line starts right at the bottom of the frame. It’ll look longer, and it’ll pull harder."},{"t":"Symmetry","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#symmetry","x":"A mountain mirrored in a still lake, split by a centre line. Here’s the one time it pays to put things in the middle. Buildings, reflections in still water and plenty of natural forms are mirror images. Centre them and the picture feels calm and formal, almost ceremonial. Symmetry only works when it’s exact. Stand on the true centre line, keep the camera level and square, and check every edge of the frame for anything that spoils the balance. Reflections are easiest early or late in the day, when the wind drops and the water goes still. Breaking a perfect symmetry can be strong too. Think of one person on an empty staircase. Water is the easiest way in. A puddle after rain can mirror a whole building if you hold the camera close to its surface. Indoors, look for corridors and arched halls, where architects have already done the work for you. Use the grid on your screen to find the exact centre, and take a moment to check that the verticals stay vertical."},{"t":"Frame within a frame","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#frame-within-a-frame","x":"A figure seen through a dark arched doorway, looking out to sea. Doorways, windows, arches, gaps in the leaves and even other people can wrap a second frame around your subject. It pulls attention inwards and adds depth. Often it also tells the viewer where you were standing. The inner frame usually works best when it’s darker than the subject, because the eye goes to the brightest part of a picture first. Expose for whatever lies beyond the frame and let the frame itself drop into shadow. It doesn’t need to be complete or sharp. A few soft branches along one edge can be enough. Frames don’t have to be architecture. Two friends standing apart can frame a third person between them, and a gap in the trees can frame a distant hill. You can even make a frame out of blur. Hold the lens close to some leaves, let them go soft, and they turn into a loose border around the sharp subject beyond."},{"t":"Negative space","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#negative-space","x":"A tiny sailboat alone on a wide, empty sea. Negative space is the empty area around a subject. Open sky, calm water, a blank wall, fog. Instead of filling every corner, you let the subject sit small inside a lot of nothing, and the emptiness becomes part of the message. It can feel calm or lonely, or show how big the world is. The empty part has to be truly empty and fairly even in tone. Place the subject off-centre, often on a third, and give it room on the side it faces or moves towards. Minimal pictures reward patience. Wait for the single boat or bird to reach the right spot. Think about where the empty space sits, not just how much of it there is. Space above a subject feels light and open. Space below can feel heavy, almost as if the subject were floating. Negative space also leaves room for words, which is why designers love these pictures for posters and covers."},{"t":"Golden spiral","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#golden-spiral","x":"A curled fern frond following a golden spiral. The golden spiral comes from the golden ratio, roughly 1 to 1.618, a proportion you’ll find in shells and in classical buildings. Laid over a picture, it curls in from one corner to a point about a third of the way in from two sides. Put your point of interest at that eye. Let lines and curves follow the spiral towards it, and the viewer gets a natural, flowing path through the frame. In practice the eye of the spiral sits close to a rule-of-thirds point. The difference is that the spiral cares about curves that lead in. Many editing programs can lay it over the crop tool, and that’s the easiest way to learn to see it. Don’t worry about matching the spiral exactly. Treat it as a reminder that the eye enjoys a curving route into a picture that ends somewhere off-centre. Natural spirals turn up in shells and curled leaves once you start looking. The overlay is a check, not a template, and the picture should still work without it."},{"t":"Diagonals","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#diagonals","x":"A staircase rising from corner to corner, with a figure near the top. Horizontal lines feel calm. Vertical lines feel strong and still. Diagonals feel like movement and tension, so a staircase, a slanting roof, a road climbing a hill or a shadow cutting from corner to corner brings energy to a picture. You can often make a diagonal just by moving. Step to the side of a straight path, or look up along a building. Be careful with a tilted horizon, though. Unless it’s clearly on purpose, people will read it as a mistake. Diagonals turn up in people too. A leaning figure or a dancer mid-jump brings the same energy as a slanting line. With sport and action, change your own position instead of tilting the camera. Shooting slightly from below often makes the motion run across the frame at an angle."},{"t":"Pattern and repetition","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#pattern-and-repetition","x":"Rows of blue beach umbrellas seen from above, with one orange umbrella breaking the pattern. Repeating shapes and colours are naturally pleasing. Rows of seats, stacked boats, windows on a facade, ripples in the sand. Fill the whole frame with a pattern so it seems to carry on past the edges and you’ve got a strong graphic picture. Then look for the odd one out. One umbrella in a different colour, or one person walking against the crowd. A single break catches the eye at once, and the pattern around it makes it stand out far more than it would alone. Shooting from above or with a longer lens flattens the pattern and cuts out distractions. Patterns hide in plain sight. Look down at paving stones and up at balconies. Markets are full of them, from stacked fruit to rolls of fabric. If you can’t find a natural break in the pattern, wait for one. A pigeon landing on a row of identical chairs can make the whole picture."},{"t":"Fill the frame","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#fill-the-frame","x":"A sunflower so close that its petals spill past every edge of the frame. Lots of beginners’ photos show the subject too small, surrounded by stuff that doesn’t matter. Filling the frame means getting closer, or zooming in, until the subject takes up most of the picture. Texture and expression become the subject. Use your feet first. Stepping closer also changes the perspective and makes the picture feel more intimate. Don’t be afraid to cut parts off at the edges. A face cropped at the forehead, or a flower spilling past the frame, can be stronger than the whole thing. Filling the frame is also a handy way to deal with a dull sky or a messy background, because you simply leave them out. With people it changes the mood completely. A tight portrait feels personal and direct, and the viewer can’t look anywhere but at the face. Try it with surfaces as well, like tree bark or peeling paint, where the texture becomes the whole subject."},{"t":"Layers of depth","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#layers-of-depth","x":"Flowers and grass in front, a cottage in the middle, mountains behind. A photograph is flat, so depth has to be suggested. One easy way is to build layers. Put something close to the camera at the front and your main subject in the middle distance, with a background behind it. The eye travels from front to back and the scene starts to feel three-dimensional. Landscape photographers often crouch low to bring flowers or rocks into the foreground. They use a wide lens and a small aperture to keep it all sharp. Overlapping shapes help, and so does haze, which makes distant hills look paler and bluer. Layers work in the city just as well. A tram in front, people in the middle and a skyline behind give the same front-to-back journey as flowers, a cottage and mountains. Keep the layers distinct with light or colour. If everything shares the same tone, the layers merge and the depth disappears."},{"t":"Point of view","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#point-of-view","x":"Looking straight up a ring of tree trunks to a patch of sky. Most photos are taken from standing eye height, which is exactly how everyone already sees the world. Change your height and you change the picture. Crouch low and small things loom large against the sky. Hold the camera right at ground level and a puddle becomes a lake. Lie down and look straight up through the trees, and the scene turns into an abstract pattern. Climb up and look down to show layout and context. Children and animals almost always look better from their own eye level. Before you press the shutter, ask yourself whether a step to the side, or a crouch, would tell the story better. The best viewpoint is often only a metre or two from where you first stood. Walk around your subject before you start shooting, and notice how the background changes with every step. A low angle can also hide a messy background behind plain sky, which is a useful trick at busy places."},{"t":"Room to move","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#room-to-move","x":"A cyclist on the left of the frame riding into open space on the right. When a subject moves or looks in one direction, viewers look that way too. Leave open space in front of it, on the side it’s heading or looking, and the movement has somewhere to go. A cyclist on the left of the frame riding right feels natural. Put the same cyclist hard against the right edge and they seem about to leave the picture. Portraits work the same way. Leave more space on the side the person faces. Break the rule on purpose, with the subject close to the edge they face, and you get tension, or a feeling that something’s been left behind. It even applies to things that don’t move. A statue gazing to the left or a tree leaning one way will feel better with space in front of it. When you crop afterwards, check which way your subject faces before you decide which side to trim. Out with the camera Combine them. Strong pictures usually use several of these at once, like a leading line that ends on a subject placed on a third. Check the edges. Before you press the shutter, run your eye around the frame for anything poking in that doesn’t belong. Take two. Make the obvious picture first. Then try a braver one from somewhere else. Turn on the grid. It costs nothing, helps with thirds and keeps your horizons straight."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Composition: the basics","u":"composition-the-basics.html#exercises","x":"1 Rule of thirds Turn on the grid. Shoot the same landscape with the horizon on the lower third, then the upper third, then right through the middle. Next, put a single tree or person on each of the four crossing points. Lay the results side by side and decide which feels strongest, and why. 2 Leading lines Find five different lines near home, like a road or a fence. For each one, find the spot where the line runs from a bottom corner straight to a subject. Then take one picture where the line leads out of the frame, and compare. 3 Symmetry Find a symmetrical building or a reflection. Stand exactly on its centre line and make the picture perfectly balanced, checking all four edges. Then take a second picture with one thing that breaks the symmetry. 4 Frame within a frame Go for a walk looking only for frames. Doorways, windows, arches, gaps in hedges, bridges. Photograph a subject through at least four different kinds. Expose for the subject and let the frame go dark. 5 Negative space On a foggy day or by calm water, photograph one small subject, like a boat or a lone walker, so it fills less than a tenth of the frame. Keep the empty area clean and put the subject on a third. 6 Golden spiral Look for natural curves, like a fern or a spiral staircase seen from above. Take a few shots, then lay the golden spiral over them in your editor’s crop tool. Crop so the curve flows into the eye of the spiral. 7 Diagonals Photograph a staircase or a sloping street twice. Once square-on, so the lines run across the frame. Once from the side, so they run corner to corner. Notice how the energy changes. 8 Pattern and repetition Find a pattern, like bicycles in a rack or cups on a shelf. Fill the frame so it seems to carry on past the edges. Then find or create one thing that breaks it, and photograph that version too. 9 Fill the frame Pick one subject, maybe a flower or a bicycle. Photograph it from where you first notice it. Take a few steps closer and shoot again, then keep going until parts are cut off. Which picture says the most? 10 Layers of depth Find a scene with something interesting in the distance. Crouch low and put a foreground element at the bottom of the frame, with your subject in the middle and the background behind. Use f/8 to f/11 to keep it all sharp. 11 Point of view Photograph one ordinary object five times. From standing height, from waist height, from the ground, from directly above and looking straight up. Pick the most surprising one. 12 Room to move Photograph a moving subject twice, a cyclist or a dog, say. Once with space in front of it and once with space behind. Do the same with someone looking off to one side. Which version feels balanced, and which feels tense?"},{"t":"Composition: going further","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html","x":"Twelve more techniques for when the basics start to feel easy. This second set goes further than just placing things in the frame. Several are about relationships, like light against dark or near against far. Others are about the light you choose and the moment you wait for. Mix them freely with the first twelve."},{"t":"Rule of odds","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#rule-of-odds","x":"Three birds perched together on a wire, with two more resting further away. Groups of three or five usually look more natural than groups of two or four. With an even number the eye pairs things up and the picture can feel static. With an odd number there’s always a middle one, so the eye keeps moving. When you arrange things yourself, like fruit on a table or stones on a beach, try three. With people or animals, wait for an odd group. It’s only a starting point, though. Two people looking at each other is a classic picture in its own right. The idea helps with details too. Three flowers in a vase usually look better than two or four, and three people in a street scene give the eye a natural triangle to follow. With bigger numbers it stops mattering. Nobody counts the birds in a flock of twenty."},{"t":"S-curve","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#s-curve","x":"A river winding in an S-shape from the foreground into the distance. An S-shaped line is one of the most graceful ways to lead the eye. A winding river, a country road, a path through a park or the curve of a shoreline carries the viewer gently from the foreground into the distance, showing the scene a bit at a time. S-curves are easiest to see from higher up, looking down along the line. A longer lens squeezes the bends together and makes the curve stronger. Start it near a bottom corner and let it fade away into the distance. Smaller S-curves are everywhere once you start looking. The neck of a swan, or a road curling down a hillside seen from a bus window. In a portrait, a relaxed pose often forms a gentle S through the body. That’s one reason it looks more natural than standing stiffly upright."},{"t":"Golden triangle","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#golden-triangle","x":"A branch running corner to corner, with a robin where a perpendicular line meets it. The golden triangle splits the frame with one diagonal from corner to corner, then two shorter lines from the other corners that meet the diagonal at right angles. You end up with triangles of different sizes and two strong points where the lines meet. It suits pictures with a strong diagonal, like a branch or a slope. Lay the main line along the long diagonal and put the subject on one of the meeting points. As with the spiral, the crop overlay in an editing program is the easiest way to learn it. It’s especially useful when a picture has two subjects. Put one on each meeting point and the diagonal links them, like a bird on a branch with the sun in the opposite corner. If the scene has no strong diagonal at all, this guide isn’t the right tool, and the rule of thirds will serve you better."},{"t":"Visual balance","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#visual-balance","x":"A big dark sea stack on the left balanced by a small red boat on the right. Balance doesn’t have to mean symmetry. Everything in a picture has visual weight. Big, dark, bright or colourful things feel heavy. Small, pale things feel light. Like kids on a seesaw, a big shape near the middle can be balanced by something small but striking further out. A big dark rock on one side and a little red boat on the other is the classic example. If a picture feels lopsided, look for something on the light side that could carry more weight. Or move until it does. Colour carries a lot of weight. A small patch of red can hold its own against a large grey area. Sharpness does too, because the eye goes to the sharpest part of a picture first. Use that when a scene feels off balance. Sometimes moving the focus, or waiting for a bright jacket to walk past, is all it takes."},{"t":"Sense of scale","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#sense-of-scale","x":"A tiny person in an orange jacket at the foot of a giant tree trunk. Mountains and waterfalls often look oddly ordinary in photos, because nothing tells the viewer how big they are. Put something of known size in the frame, usually a person, and the real scale shows at once. Keep the person small, but put them where they’ll be noticed, ideally in a contrasting colour. A red or orange jacket against green and grey stands out even when the figure is tiny. Cars and boats do the same job. Scale can work the other way round. A tiny subject looks huge when there’s nothing to compare it with, which is how a patch of moss becomes a forest and a puddle becomes a lake. Shoot from low down and keep familiar objects out of the frame. The viewer’s imagination does the rest."},{"t":"Silhouette","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#silhouette","x":"A person and a tree as black shapes against a sunset sky. A silhouette shows the subject as a pure black shape against a bright background, usually the sky at sunrise or sunset. The detail disappears, so the outline has to tell the whole story. Pick subjects with clear, recognisable shapes and keep them apart from each other. Expose for the bright sky, not the subject. Otherwise the camera brightens the subject and washes out the sky. Metering on the sky next to the sun usually works, or you can dial in some negative exposure compensation. Switch the flash off too, because it would light the subject and spoil the effect. Silhouettes are simplest against an evenly bright background. A clear sky after sunset is ideal, and so is a bright window seen from inside a dark room. Look for profiles. A face seen from the side has a clear outline, while a face seen from the front turns into a featureless shape. Hats and bicycles read well too."},{"t":"Colour contrast","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#colour-contrast","x":"An orange kayak on blue water, with a small colour wheel linking the two opposite colours. Colours opposite each other on the colour wheel are called complementary. Orange and blue, say, or red and green. Side by side they make each other look stronger, so a subject in one colour jumps out from a background in the other. Look for an orange kayak on blue water or a red door in a green street. Even a small accent can lift a muted scene. The blue hour gives you orange against blue for free on every clear evening, with warm street lights under a deep blue sky. Colour contrast doesn’t have to be loud. Muted versions work, like a dusty orange wall against a pale blue sky. Warm and cool tones side by side create contrast even when they aren’t true opposites. If a scene feels flat, ask whether one colour could be stronger, and look for the spot where two colours meet."},{"t":"Light and shadow","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#light-and-shadow","x":"A figure stepping into a shaft of sunlight in a dark passage. Light is the real subject of every photograph. When strong, directional light falls into a dark space, you get pools of brightness and deep shadow. Put your subject in the light and let the rest go dark. That gives you drama and focus, an effect painters call chiaroscuro. Find the patch of light first, then wait for someone to step into it. Expose for the bright part so it keeps its detail, and let the shadows go dark. Low sun works best, and so does light coming through a window. Shadows can be the subject as well. A long shadow stretching across a pavement can say more about a person than the person does. Early morning and late afternoon give the longest ones. Underexpose a little to keep the bright areas rich, and the shadow shapes turn into strong graphic elements of their own."},{"t":"Selective focus","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#selective-focus","x":"A sharp dandelion seed head in front of a soft, blurred green background. Selective focus keeps only your subject sharp while the background, and sometimes the foreground, melts into a soft blur. It lifts the subject off a busy background and gives a pleasing, almost three-dimensional look. Open the aperture wide, to a small f-number like f/1.8 or f/2.8, and use a longer focal length if you have one. Then get close to the subject and keep the background far away. Focus carefully. With very shallow depth of field, a few centimetres decide whether the eyes or the nose end up sharp. Phones fake this effect with a portrait mode, and it can look convincing. With a camera the real thing comes from the optics, so it’s worth learning which apertures and distances give you the look you like. In portraits, focus on the nearest eye. If that eye is sharp, viewers will accept a lot of blur everywhere else."},{"t":"Juxtaposition","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#juxtaposition","x":"A small old cottage squeezed between two tall glass towers. Juxtaposition puts two contrasting things side by side so each comments on the other. Old and new, say, or nature and the city. The meaning comes from the contrast, not from either one alone. A little old cottage squeezed between glass towers says something about a changing city that neither building could say alone. Look for pairs like that in streets and markets. You’ll often need to move around until both line up in the frame. Humour often comes from juxtaposition. A sign that says one thing while the scene shows another, or a dog that looks a lot like its owner, makes people smile because of the pairing. Keep your camera ready in busy places and watch for these small coincidences. They don’t last long."},{"t":"Eye lines","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#eye-lines","x":"A person looking up at a kite, with a dashed line following their gaze. People in a photo lead our eyes just like lines do. Viewers follow where a person is looking without thinking about it, so a gaze becomes an invisible line joining that person to something else in the frame. Use it on purpose. A child looking up at a kite works, and so do two people looking at each other. Make sure whatever they’re looking at is in the picture, or that their gaze leads into open space and not straight out of the frame. When someone looks into the camera, the eye line connects them with the viewer instead. Eye lines also reach out to the viewer. Someone looking slightly past the camera seems thoughtful, while someone looking straight into the lens seems direct and confident. In a group, watch where everyone is looking. If all eyes point at one spot, that spot becomes the centre of the picture, however small it is."},{"t":"Low horizon","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#low-horizon","x":"A big evening sky filling most of the frame above a low horizon with a small windmill. Where you put the horizon decides what the picture is about. When the sky’s the star, with dramatic clouds or a fiery sunset, drop the horizon near the bottom and give the sky most of the frame. When the land or water is more interesting, raise the horizon until the sky is a thin strip, or leave it out altogether. Keep it level. Most cameras have a level indicator for exactly this. A horizon straight through the middle often makes a picture feel undecided, unless you’re shooting a reflection. A high horizon suits patterns on the ground, like ploughed fields or a busy beach seen from above. A very low one suits big weather and night skies. Decide before you shoot what the picture is about. Then place the horizon to match, and commit to it."},{"t":"Putting it all together","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#putting-it-all-together","x":"Very few strong photographs rely on one technique. A classic landscape might have a low horizon, an S-curve coming in from a corner, layers of depth and warm evening light, all at once. A street photo might use light and shadow, with a figure on a third and room to move in front of them. The techniques help each other. A leading line works better when it ends on a subject you’ve placed with care. When a scene catches your eye, stop and ask what drew you to it. A shape, the light, a colour, a face? Then pick the techniques that make that one thing stronger, and get rid of anything competing with it. Before you press the shutter What’s the subject? If you can’t say in a few words what the picture is about, the viewer won’t know either. Where does the eye go first? It should land on your subject, not on a bright patch or a sign at the edge. What can go? Step closer, zoom in or change angle until nothing in the frame is wasted. Is there a better spot? Try one step left, right, up or down before you move on."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Composition: going further","u":"composition-going-further.html#exercises","x":"13 Rule of odds Arrange two, then three, four and five similar objects, like apples or stones, and photograph each group from the same spot. Which numbers feel most natural? Then go looking for groups of three out in the street. 14 S-curve Find a winding road or river and photograph it from the highest point you can reach. Try a wide focal length and a longer one, and see which makes the curve stronger. 15 Golden triangle Find a strong diagonal, like a branch or a leaning post. Lay it along the corner-to-corner diagonal and put a subject where one of the short lines meets it. Check with the crop overlay afterwards. 16 Visual balance Find a big, heavy element, such as a building or a dark tree, and a small bright one, such as a person or a red sign. Move until the small one balances the big one from the opposite side of the frame. 17 Sense of scale Photograph something huge twice, a tree or a cliff, say. Once on its own and once with a person next to it. A friend in a bright jacket helps. 18 Silhouette At sunrise or sunset, put a person or a tree against the bright sky. Meter on the sky, or dial in −1 to −2 EV, so the subject turns black. Make sure the outline is clear and stands apart from everything else. 19 Colour contrast Go on a colour hunt for complementary pairs, like orange and blue or red and green. Photograph five examples. Add one more where a small accent of colour lifts an otherwise dull scene. 20 Light and shadow Find a patch of strong light in a dark area. A gap between buildings works, or sun through trees. Set the exposure for the light and wait for someone to step into it. 21 Selective focus Use your widest aperture, the smallest f-number, and a longer focal length. Photograph a flower or a person with the background as far away as you can get it. Repeat at f/11 and compare the backgrounds. 22 Juxtaposition Look for pairs that tell a story together. Old and new, big and small, nature and concrete, serious and funny. Find a viewpoint where both show clearly in one frame. 23 Eye lines Photograph someone looking at something, a book or a view, and include what they’re looking at. Then photograph them looking out of the frame, and compare. 24 Low horizon On a day with a dramatic sky, shoot the same view with the horizon near the bottom, through the middle and near the top. Do it again on a dull day and see which placement works best then."},{"t":"Reading the histogram","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html","x":"Your camera’s most honest exposure tool, and how to read it at a glance. The screen on the back of a camera is a poor judge of exposure. In bright sun it looks too dark. In a dim room it looks too bright, and it may not match your computer at all. The histogram is far more reliable. It’s a graph of how bright every pixel in the picture is, and once you can read it you’ll know straight away whether you’ve lost detail."},{"t":"How to read it","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html#how-to-read-it","x":"The histogram of the well-exposed lake photo below, split into its five tonal zones. The horizontal axis runs from pure black on the left to pure white on the right. The height of the graph at each point shows how many pixels have that brightness. A tall peak in the middle means lots of midtones, and a long, low tail on the right means a few bright highlights. It tells you nothing about where those pixels are in the picture. Only how many of each tone there are. Photographers split the range into five zones. Blacks sit at the far left. They’re the deepest tones, where detail disappears. Shadows are dark areas that still show texture. Midtones hold most of a typical scene, things like grass and skin. Highlights are bright areas with detail left in them, and Whites, at the far right, are the brightest tones, where detail disappears again. Most editing programs use the same names for their tone sliders, so learning them pays off twice."},{"t":"Clipping, and why the edges matter","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html#clipping-and-why-the-edges-matter","x":"If the graph is pressed against either edge, some pixels were recorded as pure black or pure white. That’s called clipping. Those pixels hold no detail at all, and no amount of editing will bring it back. A little clipping is fine, in the sun itself or a sparkle on water. Big clipped areas in a face or a cloudy sky usually aren’t. Clipped shadows are a bit more forgiving than clipped highlights. Modern sensors keep a lot of information in dark areas, and lifting shadows in editing is far easier than rescuing burnt-out whites. That’s why many photographers expose to protect the highlights and brighten everything else later."},{"t":"What the shape tells you","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html#what-the-shape-tells-you","x":"Balanced Tones spread across the whole range and nothing piles up at either edge. Shadows keep their texture and highlights keep their detail. A typical, evenly lit scene, exposed correctly. Underexposed Most of the data is crowded on the left and the darkest areas are clipped to black. The picture looks dark and muddy, and brightening it later adds noise. Add light with positive exposure compensation or a slower shutter. A wider aperture or a higher ISO works too. Overexposed The data piles up against the right edge. The sky has gone pure white and that detail is gone for good. Use negative exposure compensation or a faster shutter speed, then check the histogram again. Low contrast A narrow hump with gaps at both ends, so nothing is truly dark or truly bright. Fog and flat overcast light make this shape. It can look lovely and moody. If it doesn’t, add contrast in editing by pulling the black and white points in towards the data."},{"t":"There’s no perfect shape","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html#theres-no-perfect-shape","x":"A correctly exposed photo of snow should have most of its data on the right. A night street should have most of it on the left. So judge the histogram against the scene in front of you, not against some ideal hill in the middle, and ask whether you’ve lost any detail you actually care about."},{"t":"Expose to protect the highlights","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html#expose-to-protect-the-highlights","x":"Lost highlight detail can’t be rescued, so a common approach is to make the exposure as bright as you can without clipping anything important. The data sits towards the right of the histogram but stops short of the edge. People call this exposing to the right. You get cleaner shadows, because brightening dark areas in editing adds noise and darkening bright areas doesn’t. Keep an eye on the highlight warning. If the bright areas that matter start to flash, take away a third or two-thirds of a stop."},{"t":"The histogram in editing","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html#the-histogram-in-editing","x":"Editing programs show the same graph, and their main tone sliders act on the same five zones. Moving the Whites slider right stretches the data towards the right edge. Moving Blacks left anchors the darkest tones. Many programs can show clipped areas in colour while you drag. Watch the graph as you edit, not just the picture, because even a good screen can hide a clipped sky."},{"t":"Colour channels","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html#colour-channels","x":"Many cameras can also show separate histograms for the red, green and blue channels. The overall graph can look perfectly fine while one colour is clipped. It happens a lot with red flowers and stage lighting, where a single channel runs into the right edge long before the others. When that happens the colour loses its texture and turns into a flat patch. Taking away a third of a stop usually brings the detail back."},{"t":"When a scene is too contrasty","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html#when-a-scene-is-too-contrasty","x":"Some scenes hold a wider range of brightness than the sensor can record. A sunny window seen from inside a dark room is a typical example, and so is a portrait lit from behind. The histogram shows it straight away, with data pressed against both edges at once. No single exposure can fix that. You’ve got a few ways out. Protect the highlights and let the shadows go black, which can look dramatic. Add light to the shadows with a reflector or a flash. Wait for a cloud, or for the softer light of evening. Or take several exposures and blend them later, which many cameras and phones do automatically in their HDR mode. Which one you pick depends on what matters most in the picture. In the field Show it every time. Turn on the histogram in playback, or live in the viewfinder if your camera has that. Turn on the blinkies. The highlight warning makes clipped areas flash in the review image, so you see exactly where detail is lost. Check the colour channels. A red flower or a sunset can clip the red channel even when the overall histogram looks fine. Remember the preview. The camera’s histogram comes from a JPEG preview, so a raw file usually holds a bit more highlight detail than it shows. Trust the histogram, NOT your display. Screens lie in bright sun and in the dark. The graph doesn’t."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Reading the histogram","u":"reading-the-histogram.html#exercises","x":"H1 Predict, then look Turn on the histogram. Before you look at each picture, read the histogram and guess how the photo will look. Then check whether you were right. H2 Bracket a scene Shoot one scene at −2, −1, 0, +1 and +2 EV. Compare the five histograms and note the step where clipping starts on each side. H3 Snow and night Make a correct-looking exposure of a very bright scene and of a very dark one. The histograms lean right and left, and that’s exactly how they should look. H4 Chase the blinkies Turn on the highlight warning and photograph a bright sky above a dark foreground. Lower the exposure a third of a stop at a time until the flashing stops. Note how much you had to take away."},{"t":"Light as temperature","c":"Light as temperature","u":"light-as-temperature.html","x":"What the kelvin numbers mean, and how white balance uses them. Light has colour. Candlelight is orange, and the shade on a clear day is blue. Our eyes and brain adjust so smoothly that we barely notice, and a sheet of white paper looks white to us whatever the light. A camera has no such instinct. You have to tell it the colour of the light so it can make white things white. That setting is white balance."},{"t":"The Kelvin scale","c":"Light as temperature","u":"light-as-temperature.html#the-kelvin-scale","x":"Colour temperature is measured in kelvin, written K, a unit borrowed from physics. Heat a dark object and it starts to glow. First deep red, then orange and yellow, then white and finally bluish white. The temperature at which it glows a given colour gives that colour its number. That’s why the scale feels backwards. Warm-looking orange light has a low number, around 2,000 K, while cool-looking blue light sits at 7,000 K and above. Clear blue sky 10,000 K and up Kelvin 10,000 Open shade 7,500–8,000 K Shade Overcast sky 6,500–7,500 K Cloudy Electronic flash 5,500–6,000 K Flash Midday sun 5,000–5,500 K Daylight Fluorescent tube 4,000–4,500 K White fluorescent Sunrise and sunset 2,500–3,500 K Cloudy keeps the glow Household bulb 2,700–3,000 K Tungsten Candle flame about 1,900 K Kelvin 2,500 (lowest)"},{"t":"How white balance works","c":"Light as temperature","u":"light-as-temperature.html#how-white-balance-works","x":"When you pick a white balance setting, you’re telling the camera what light you’re standing in. It then adds the opposite colour to cancel the cast. Set Tungsten, about 3,200 K, and it adds blue to neutralise the orange of a light bulb. Set Shade, about 7,000 K, and it adds amber to cancel the blue. The pictures below show one sunny scene at different settings. At 3,200 K the camera adds blue that sunlight didn’t need, and everything turns cold. 5,200 K matches the sun, so whites stay white and colours look true. At 8,000 K the camera adds amber and the whole scene turns warm. It also explains something that confuses lots of people in Kelvin mode. A higher number makes the picture warmer, and a lower one makes it cooler. You aren’t choosing the colour of the picture. You’re telling the camera the colour of the light."},{"t":"Auto, presets and Kelvin mode","c":"Light as temperature","u":"light-as-temperature.html#auto-presets-and-kelvin-mode","x":"Auto white balance copes well with most daylight and indoor light, roughly from 3,000 to 7,000 K. Outside that range, and in mixed light, it’s easily fooled. Candlelight stays too orange and deep shade too blue. A sunset loses its glow, because Auto tries to neutralise it. Every camera also has presets. The names and exact values vary a little from one maker to the next, but you’ll typically find Tungsten at about 3,000–3,200 K, White fluorescent at about 4,000 K, Daylight at about 5,200 K, Flash at about 5,400–6,000 K, Cloudy at about 6,000 K and Shade at about 7,000–8,000 K. Kelvin mode lets you dial in a number directly, usually anywhere from 2,500 to 10,000 K."},{"t":"Using white balance creatively","c":"Light as temperature","u":"light-as-temperature.html#using-white-balance-creatively","x":"White balance is a creative tool as much as a fix. At sunset, Daylight or Cloudy keeps the warm glow that Auto would strip away. Set it low on purpose in daylight for a cold, wintry mood, or a little higher to warm up the skin in a portrait. At blue hour, Daylight keeps the sky a rich blue and makes the street lights glow orange."},{"t":"Light through the day","c":"Light as temperature","u":"light-as-temperature.html#light-through-the-day","x":"Daylight changes colour from hour to hour. Just before sunrise the world looks cool and blue. As the sun comes up the light turns golden, then grows more neutral towards midday. Late in the afternoon it warms again, and after sunset comes the blue hour, when the sky glows deep blue for twenty minutes or so. Photographers plan around these times, because the same street can look like a different place at eight in the morning and at noon."},{"t":"Artificial light","c":"Light as temperature","u":"light-as-temperature.html#artificial-light","x":"Indoor light varies far more than daylight. Old-style bulbs give a warm orange glow. Fluorescent tubes and LED lamps can be warm or cool, and some add a slight green or magenta tint that no kelvin setting can fully remove. That’s what the Tint slider is for. Mixed sources are the hardest case. A room lit by a window and a table lamp has two colours of light at once, and the camera can only balance for one of them."},{"t":"Correcting white balance afterwards","c":"Light as temperature","u":"light-as-temperature.html#correcting-white-balance-afterwards","x":"If you shoot raw, white balance is just a setting stored with the file. You can change it as much as you like in editing and lose nothing. Most programs give you two sliders. Temperature moves the colour between blue and amber, along the same scale as the kelvin numbers. Tint moves it between green and magenta, which helps under fluorescent tubes and cheap LED lamps, because their light often has a green cast. The quickest fix is the white balance picker. Click it on something that should be neutral, like a grey wall or the grey card you photographed earlier, and the program sets both sliders for you. JPEG files are different. The camera has already baked its white balance in, so big corrections are possible but colours can start to look a bit off. Getting it close in the camera is always the better start. Getting colour right Use a grey card. Photograph a neutral grey card in the same light and set a custom white balance from it when colours have to be exact. Check the skin. Faces are the toughest test of white balance. If skin looks right, the rest usually does too."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Light as temperature","u":"light-as-temperature.html#exercises","x":"W1 Every preset Photograph one scene with every white balance preset your camera has. Which looks most natural? Which looks most interesting? W2 Kelvin walk In Kelvin mode, photograph a scene at 3,000, 5,000 and 8,000 K. Then find the value that makes a white wall look truly white. W3 Mixed light Photograph a room lit by both a window and a lamp. Balance once for each light and decide which suits the subject. W4 Keep the glow Photograph a sunset on Auto, then again on Daylight or Cloudy. Which one keeps the warmth you saw?"},{"t":"Quality and direction of light","c":"Quality and direction of light","u":"quality-and-direction-of-light.html","x":"Hard or soft, from the front or from behind. How light shapes what you photograph. Chapter 4 was about the colour of light. This one is about its shape. Two things matter most, how hard or soft the light is and which direction it comes from. Learn to read both and you’ll start choosing the time and place for a photo by its light, not just by its subject."},{"t":"Hard and soft light","c":"Quality and direction of light","u":"quality-and-direction-of-light.html#hard-and-soft-light","x":"Hard light · crisp shadow Soft light · gentle shadow Hard light comes from a small, bright source, like the midday sun on a clear day or a bare bulb. Its shadows have crisp edges and the contrast is high. Textures jump out, and so do wrinkles and blemishes. Soft light comes from a large source, or from light that has been spread out. An overcast sky turns the whole sky into one huge lamp, and a big north-facing window does the same indoors. Shadows fade gently from dark to light, and faces look smooth and even. What counts is the size of the source as the subject sees it. The sun is enormous, but it’s so far away that it acts like a tiny point. Move a lamp closer to a face and its light gets softer, because it now looks bigger from the face’s point of view. Move it away and the light hardens again."},{"t":"Which way the light falls","c":"Quality and direction of light","u":"quality-and-direction-of-light.html#which-way-the-light-falls","x":"Front light. Bright colours, flat look. Side light. Shape and texture stand out. Back light. A glowing rim, a darker front. Top light. Short shadows, hard on faces. Front light comes from behind the camera and falls straight onto the subject. Colours look bright and clean, but the shadows hide behind the subject, so the picture can look flat. It suits bold colours and graphic shapes. Side light comes from roughly ninety degrees to one side. Half the subject is lit and half falls into shadow, which shows shape and texture better than any other direction. Low evening sun raking across a field is the classic example, and so is a window beside a face. Back light comes from behind the subject towards the camera. It outlines hair and leaves with a glowing rim and makes petals light up from inside. Expose for the sky and you get a silhouette. Add a little exposure if you want the front of the subject to keep its detail, and watch out for flare. Light from straight above, like the sun at midday, is the least flattering for people. It drops dark shadows into the eye sockets and under the nose. If you have to shoot at noon, look for open shade, or turn the person so the light reaches them from one side."},{"t":"The golden hour and the blue hour","c":"Quality and direction of light","u":"quality-and-direction-of-light.html#the-golden-hour-and-the-blue-hour","x":"The hour after sunrise and the hour before sunset are called the golden hour. The sun is low, so the light is warm and comes in from the side, and shadows stretch out long. It’s the easiest time of day to make almost anything look good. The blue hour follows sunset, when the sun has gone but the sky still glows deep blue. Weather and planning apps show both times for any place and date."},{"t":"Shaping light yourself","c":"Quality and direction of light","u":"quality-and-direction-of-light.html#shaping-light-yourself","x":"You don’t need a studio to control light. A white card or a cheap folding reflector bounces light back into the shadows. Hold it below a face and the dark patches under the eyes soften. A thin white curtain turns hard sunlight through a window into soft light. A black card on the shadow side does the opposite and deepens the shadows for a moodier look."},{"t":"Fill flash","c":"Quality and direction of light","u":"quality-and-direction-of-light.html#fill-flash","x":"A small flash isn’t only for the dark. In bright sun, a touch of flash fills in harsh shadows on a face, or lifts a subject standing in front of a bright background. Most cameras have a flash exposure compensation setting. Turn it down to about −1 or −2 so the flash fills the shadows without looking like flash. Bouncing the flash off a white ceiling or wall makes the source bigger, and so the light gets softer. Reading the light Look at the shadows first. Their edges tell you straight away whether the light is hard or soft. Walk around the subject. The same light looks completely different from the front and from behind. Use the clock. Golden hour light can turn an ordinary street into a picture. Carry a reflector. A white card in your bag is the cheapest lighting kit there is."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Quality and direction of light","u":"quality-and-direction-of-light.html#exercises","x":"L1 Hard and soft Photograph the same object, like a piece of fruit, on a sunny windowsill and again on a cloudy day or in shade. Compare the edges of the shadows and how much texture shows. L2 Walk around the light With the sun low, photograph a friend or a statue four times as you walk around them, a quarter turn each time. Note how the shape and the mood change. L3 Golden hour Photograph one place at midday and again in the golden hour. Lay the two pictures side by side. L4 Reflector Photograph a face lit from one side by a window, first as it is and then with a white card bouncing light into the shadow side."},{"t":"Metering modes","c":"Metering modes","u":"metering-modes.html","x":"How your camera decides how bright a photo should be. Before every shot, your camera measures the light and picks an exposure. It does this with a built-in light meter, and the metering mode decides which part of the frame the meter pays attention to. Once you know what the meter is trying to do, and how it gets fooled, exposure stops being guesswork."},{"t":"How a meter thinks","c":"Metering modes","u":"metering-modes.html#how-a-meter-thinks","x":"A camera meter measures the light bouncing off the scene, not the light falling on it. It can’t tell a white wall in dim light from a grey wall in bright light, because both reflect the same amount. So it makes an assumption. It treats whatever it reads as a middle tone, the grey of a card that reflects 18% of the light, and picks the exposure that would make that area middle grey. Three scenes and what an averaging meter makes of them. For most everyday scenes, with a mix of light and dark, that works fine. A frame full of snow is much brighter than middle grey, though, so the meter darkens it to a dull grey. A black cat on a dark sofa is much darker, so the meter brightens it to a washed-out grey. Exposure compensation fixes both. Add about +1 to +2 EV for snow or white walls, and take away 1 to 2 EV for dark subjects."},{"t":"The four metering modes","c":"Metering modes","u":"metering-modes.html#the-four-metering-modes","x":"Cameras offer several metering modes. The names change from maker to maker but the ideas don’t. To show the difference, here’s one scene metered four ways. A singer stands in a spotlight on a dark stage, slightly off-centre, with the focus point on the face. Evaluative / matrix The camera splits the frame into many zones and compares the pattern of brightness with a library of typical scenes. It gives extra weight to the zone around the active focus point. It’s the best all-round mode and handles most situations well. Here, though, the big dark hall still drags the exposure up, so the singer comes out too bright and the white shirt burns out. Centre-weighted Measures the whole frame but gives most of the weight, often about three-quarters, to a large circle in the middle. It’s predictable, which is why plenty of portrait photographers like it. Here the circle covers mostly dark stage. The meter brightens everything and the singer ends up completely overexposed. Spot Measures only a tiny circle, about 1.5–3% of the frame, either in the centre or at the active focus point. You get full control. Point it at whatever has to be right. On the singer’s face it nails the exposure, and the hall stays dark. Highlight-weighted Some cameras have this one. It sets the exposure so the brightest parts of the frame keep their detail, which makes it ideal for spotlit performers and other bright subjects on dark backgrounds. Here it holds the white shirt and the face, and everything else stays dark, the way it should."},{"t":"Exposure compensation and lock","c":"Metering modes","u":"metering-modes.html#exposure-compensation-and-lock","x":"In the automatic and semi-automatic modes, exposure compensation is how you tell the camera its meter got it wrong. It’s usually a dial or a button marked plus and minus, and it moves in thirds of a stop. +1 makes the picture one stop brighter than the meter wants. −1 makes it one stop darker. Set it back to zero afterwards, or the next scene will be off too. Exposure lock, often marked AE-L, freezes the meter reading. Point the camera at the part that has to be right, lock it, then recompose. That’s the classic way to use spot metering. Meter on the face, lock, and frame the picture however you like."},{"t":"Reflected and incident light","c":"Metering modes","u":"metering-modes.html#reflected-and-incident-light","x":"Every camera meter reads reflected light, which is exactly why bright and dark subjects can fool it. A handheld light meter can also measure incident light. You hold it at the subject, pointing back towards the camera, and it reads the light falling on the scene. That reading doesn’t care how bright or dark the subject is, so snow stays white and a black cat stays black. Studio photographers still use them, and a grey card gives you much the same benefit for free."},{"t":"Metering in practice","c":"Metering modes","u":"metering-modes.html#metering-in-practice","x":"In everyday shooting, most photographers leave the camera on evaluative metering and keep an eye on the histogram. When a scene tricks the meter, they reach for exposure compensation first. Spot metering comes out for the awkward cases, like a performer under a spotlight or a face against a bright window. Once you know how your own camera reacts, you’ll find yourself dialling in compensation before you’ve even taken the first shot."},{"t":"Which mode when?","c":"Metering modes","u":"metering-modes.html#which-mode-when","x":"Everyday scenes Evaluative / matrix Concert or stage Spot or highlight-weighted Backlit portrait Spot on the face Sunset, bright sky Highlight-weighted, or −1 EV Snow or beach Evaluative, +1 to +2 EV Making the meter work for you Lock it. Meter on your subject, hold the exposure lock or keep the shutter button half pressed, then recompose. Check it. The histogram shows whether the meter was fooled. Adjust with exposure compensation and shoot again. Know where spot reads. Some cameras spot-meter at the active focus point, others only in the centre. Find out which yours does. Use it in manual too. The meter still guides you in manual mode. Start at 0 on the exposure scale, then adjust by eye."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Metering modes","u":"metering-modes.html#exercises","x":"M1 Middle grey Fill the frame with a white wall, then a grey surface, then a black one, all on automatic exposure. They all come out grey. Fix them with exposure compensation. M2 Four modes Find a scene with strong contrast, like a person against a bright window. Photograph it in each metering mode without compensation and compare. M3 Lock and recompose Spot-meter a face, lock the exposure, recompose and shoot. Compare it with a picture taken without locking. M4 Compensate by eye Shoot a white flower or a pale beach on automatic exposure. Add compensation a third of a stop at a time until it looks the way you remember it. Then set it back to zero."},{"t":"The exposure triangle","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html","x":"The settings behind every exposure, and how they trade off. Every exposure comes down to the aperture, the shutter speed and the ISO. Together they decide how bright the picture is. But each one also changes the look. Aperture changes how much is sharp from front to back, shutter speed changes how motion looks, and ISO changes how much noise you get. Balancing them is what shooting in manual is all about. Think of exposure as filling a glass under a tap. The aperture is how far you open the tap and the shutter speed is how long you let it run. ISO decides how full the glass has to be before it counts as full. At a higher ISO, less water will do. Open the tap wide for a moment or a little for longer and you collect the same amount, but the picture looks different. Aperture. The opening in the lens. A wider opening lets in more light and blurs the background. Shutter speed. How long the sensor collects light. Faster freezes motion, slower blurs it. ISO. How much the signal is amplified. Higher brightens the picture and adds grain."},{"t":"Stops, the common currency","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#stops-the-common-currency","x":"Photographers measure light in stops. One stop means double or half the light. Open the aperture by one stop, or use a shutter speed twice as long, or double the ISO, and the picture gets one stop brighter. Because they all use the same unit you can trade one for another. Open the aperture a stop, make the shutter a stop faster, and the brightness stays exactly the same while the look changes."},{"t":"Aperture","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#aperture","x":"f/1.8 · soft background f/11 · all sharp The aperture is the adjustable opening inside the lens. It’s described by an f-number, which is the focal length divided by the width of the opening. That’s why a smaller number means a bigger opening. The standard full stops are f/1.4, f/2, f/2.8, f/4, f/5.6, f/8, f/11, f/16 and f/22, and each one lets in half the light of the one before. Aperture also controls depth of field, the zone from near to far that looks sharp. A wide aperture like f/1.8 gives shallow depth of field. The subject is sharp and the background melts away, which is lovely for portraits. A small aperture like f/11 keeps everything sharp from the foreground to the horizon, which suits landscapes. Most lenses are sharpest two or three stops down from their widest setting. At very small apertures such as f/22, an effect called diffraction softens everything slightly."},{"t":"Shutter speed","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#shutter-speed","x":"1/15 s · motion blur 1/1000 s · frozen Shutter speed is how long the sensor collects light. It’s written in seconds or fractions of a second, so 1/1000, 1/500, 1/250, 1/125, 1/60 and so on, each one twice as long as the one before. Fast speeds freeze motion and slow speeds blur it. Roughly speaking, 1/500 s freezes people walking and cycling, and 1/1000 s or faster freezes sport and birds in flight. Anything slower than about 1/60 s risks blur from your own hands. Blur isn’t always bad. A slow shutter turns a waterfall to silk and car lights into streaks. If you follow a moving subject at around 1/30 s, it stays sharp against a streaky background. For hand-held shots, keep the shutter speed at least one over the focal length, so 1/50 s at 50 mm. Image stabilisation lets you go slower."},{"t":"ISO","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#iso","x":"ISO 6400 · grainy ISO 100 · clean ISO sets how much the signal from the sensor gets amplified. Double it from 100 to 200 and the picture gets one stop brighter without touching the aperture or the shutter. The price is noise, those grainy speckles and blotches of colour that show up most in dark areas. The base ISO, usually 100, gives the cleanest result. Modern cameras give very usable pictures at ISO 1600 to 6400, and noise reduction in editing helps a lot. So raise the ISO when you need a faster shutter or a smaller aperture than the light allows. Just raise it last. A sharp photo with some grain almost always beats a clean, blurry one."},{"t":"Depth of field in more detail","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#depth-of-field-in-more-detail","x":"Aperture isn’t the only thing that controls depth of field. Distance matters just as much. The closer you get to the subject, the thinner the zone of sharpness becomes, which is why close-ups of flowers can look soft even at f/8. A longer focal length makes the background look softer too. So does a bigger sensor at the same framing, and that’s why phones, with their tiny sensors, keep almost everything sharp. When you want everything sharp in a landscape, focus about a third of the way into the scene instead of on the horizon. At f/8 to f/11 with a wide lens, that usually covers everything from a few metres in front of you to the far distance."},{"t":"Every stop on one scale","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#every-stop-on-one-scale","x":"Aperture f/1.4 f/2 f/2.8 f/4 f/5.6 f/8 f/11 f/16 Blurry background Sharp throughout Shutter 1/8 1/15 1/30 1/60 1/125 1/250 1/500 1/1000 Motion blur Frozen motion ISO 12800 6400 3200 1600 800 400 200 100 More grain Clean Read the scale across. Every column lets in the same amount of light, so f/2.8 at 1/500 s, f/4 at 1/250 s and f/5.6 at 1/125 s all give the same exposure. So does f/4 at 1/500 s with the ISO doubled. Pick the combination whose look you want. A soft background or a sharp one. Frozen motion or flowing motion."},{"t":"Exposure value","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#exposure-value","x":"You’ll often see exposure written as EV, short for exposure value. One EV is the same as one stop. When the camera shows +1 EV, it means one stop more light than the meter wanted. EV is also used to describe how bright a scene is. A sunny beach sits around EV 15 at ISO 100, while a dim living room is closer to EV 5."},{"t":"Let the camera help","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#let-the-camera-help","x":"You don’t have to set everything yourself. In aperture priority, often marked A or Av on the mode dial, you pick the aperture and the camera picks the shutter speed. Shutter priority, S or Tv, works the other way round. With auto ISO, many cameras let you set a minimum shutter speed, so the ISO only climbs when it has to. Manual mode, M, gives you full control, with the meter as a guide."},{"t":"Where to start","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#where-to-start","x":"Portrait f/2.8 1/250 s ISO 100 Soft background Landscape f/8 1/125 s ISO 100 Sharp front to back Action f/4 1/1000 s ISO 400 Freeze the moment Concert f/2 1/125 s ISO 3200 Fast lens, high ISO Show movement f/11 1/15 s ISO 100 Brace the camera City at night f/8 10 s ISO 100 Tripod, no grain Things to remember Decide what matters first. Pick the setting whose look matters most, then balance the other two around it. Hand-held rule. Keep the shutter at least one over the focal length, so 1/50 s at 50 mm and 1/200 s at 200 mm."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"The exposure triangle","u":"the-exposure-triangle.html#exercises","x":"E1 Aperture In aperture priority, photograph a subject a metre away with the background far behind. Shoot it at your widest aperture, then f/4, f/8 and f/16. Compare the backgrounds. E2 Shutter speed In shutter priority, photograph moving water or a friend running at 1/1000, 1/125, 1/30 and 1/8 s. Compare how the motion looks. E3 ISO In a dim room, take the same picture at ISO 100 on a tripod and at ISO 3200 or 6400 hand-held. Zoom in and compare the noise. E4 Trade stops In manual mode, find a correct exposure. Then make more pictures of the same brightness using other combinations from the stop scale, and describe how each one looks."},{"t":"Focus","c":"Focus","u":"focus.html","x":"Getting the right thing sharp, and keeping it sharp. A photo can survive a slightly wrong exposure. It rarely survives the wrong thing being in focus. Modern autofocus is fast and clever, but it still needs you to tell it what matters. This chapter explains the main settings and when to use them."},{"t":"Single and continuous autofocus","c":"Focus","u":"focus.html#single-and-continuous-autofocus","x":"Most cameras offer two main autofocus modes. In single autofocus, often labelled AF-S, the camera focuses once when you half press the shutter and then locks. It’s the right choice for still subjects like landscapes and posed portraits. In continuous autofocus, often labelled AF-C, the camera keeps adjusting focus for as long as you hold the button down. Use it for anything that moves, from children and pets to sport and birds. Many cameras also have an automatic mode that switches between the two. It’s handy, but less predictable."},{"t":"Focus points and areas","c":"Focus","u":"focus.html#focus-points-and-areas","x":"Single point. You choose exactly where to focus. Zone. The camera picks within a group of points. Wide area. The camera searches the whole frame. Eye detection. It finds and follows the nearest eye. You also choose where in the frame the camera looks for focus. A single point gives you exact control, and you move it onto the subject’s eye or whatever spot matters. Zone and wide-area modes let the camera choose within a larger area, which works well for a moving subject against a plain background. Many recent cameras can also recognise eyes, faces, animals and vehicles and follow them around the frame. Focus and recompose is the older way of working. You put the centre point on the subject, half press, then move the camera to frame the picture. It works well at small apertures. At very wide apertures and close distances, though, that small movement can shift the plane of focus enough to soften the eyes. Moving the focus point is safer then."},{"t":"Back-button focus","c":"Focus","u":"focus.html#back-button-focus","x":"Many photographers separate focusing from taking the picture. They set a button on the back of the camera to start autofocus and leave the shutter button to fire the shutter. In continuous mode, holding the back button tracks the subject. Letting go locks focus, so you can recompose freely without the camera refocusing. It takes about a week to get used to, and then most people never go back."},{"t":"Manual focus","c":"Focus","u":"focus.html#manual-focus","x":"Some scenes confuse autofocus. Very low light and low-contrast subjects, like fog or a plain wall, are hard for it. So is a subject behind something closer, like a bird behind branches. Switch to manual focus there. Most cameras help with magnification, which enlarges part of the view, and with focus peaking, which outlines sharp edges in colour."},{"t":"Where to focus","c":"Focus","u":"focus.html#where-to-focus","x":"In portraits, focus on the eye nearest the camera. With a group, focus on someone in the middle row and use a smaller aperture so the front and back rows stay sharp. In landscapes, focus about a third of the way into the scene, as chapter 7 explains. Depth of field runs roughly one third in front of the focus point and two thirds behind it. The hyperfocal distance is the closest point you can focus on while the far distance still looks acceptably sharp. Focus there and everything from half that distance to the horizon looks sharp. Apps and tables give the exact figure for your lens and aperture, but the one-third rule gets you most of the way."},{"t":"Sharpness isn’t only focus","c":"Focus","u":"focus.html#sharpness-isnt-only-focus","x":"A soft picture isn’t always a focus problem. Camera shake blurs everything a little in one direction, while subject movement blurs only the subject. A dirty lens or a cheap filter lowers contrast. Zoom in on your photo and look for the sharpest spot. If nothing is sharp, suspect shake. If something in front of or behind the subject is sharp, the focus missed. Sharp results Match the mode to the subject. Single for still subjects, continuous for anything that moves. Put the point on the eye. Viewers forgive a soft background, but not soft eyes. Check at 100%. Zoom right in on the camera screen after an important shot. Hold steady. Brace your elbows against your body and press the shutter gently."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Focus","u":"focus.html#exercises","x":"A1 Single and continuous Photograph a friend walking towards you, first in single autofocus and then in continuous. Count how many shots are sharp in each set. A2 Point on the eye Take a portrait at your widest aperture with the focus point on the nearest eye. Repeat with the point on the nose and compare. A3 Manual focus at night Focus by hand on a distant street light using magnification. Check the result at 100% and try again until it’s crisp. A4 What went soft? Go through your recent soft photos and work out for each one what made it soft. Keep a note of the most common cause."},{"t":"Filters","c":"Filters","u":"filters.html","x":"Glass in front of the lens that does what editing can’t. Most filters from the film days have been replaced by editing. A few still matter, because they change the light before it reaches the sensor, and no slider can undo that. They come as round filters that screw onto the front of the lens, or as square ones that slide into a holder."},{"t":"Polarising filters","c":"Filters","u":"filters.html#polarising-filters","x":"Without a polariser With a polariser A circular polariser cuts reflected light. Turn it while you look through the camera and you’ll see reflections on water and glass fade away, so you can look into a lake or through a shop window. It also takes the shine off leaves, which makes foliage look richer, and it darkens a blue sky. The effect on the sky is strongest when the sun is at ninety degrees to the direction you’re shooting. With a very wide lens that angle changes across the frame, so the sky can turn patchy, darker on one side than the other. A polariser also costs you one or two stops of light."},{"t":"Neutral density filters","c":"Filters","u":"filters.html#neutral-density-filters","x":"1/250 s · no filter 30 s · ten-stop ND filter A neutral density filter, or ND filter, is simply dark grey glass. It cuts light without changing colours, so you can use slower shutter speeds or wider apertures in bright conditions. A three-stop ND turns 1/125 s into about 1/15 s. A ten-stop ND turns 1/30 s into about 30 seconds, long enough to smooth water into mist and make clouds streak across the sky. Focus and compose before you put on a strong ND, because the view through the camera goes very dark. Use a tripod and a two-second timer. Cover the viewfinder eyepiece too if your camera has one, since stray light leaking in can show up as haze."},{"t":"Graduated filters","c":"Filters","u":"filters.html#graduated-filters","x":"A graduated ND filter is dark at the top and clear at the bottom. Line up the transition with the horizon and it holds back a bright sky while leaving the land untouched, so both fit into one exposure. Hard-edged versions suit flat horizons like the sea. Soft-edged ones suit hills and trees. Many photographers now get the same result by bracketing exposures and blending them in editing."},{"t":"Protective filters","c":"Filters","u":"filters.html#protective-filters","x":"A clear or UV filter on the front of the lens mostly works as a shield against scratches and sea spray. Digital sensors don’t need UV filtering. If you use one, pick a good one, because a cheap filter adds flare and softens the picture. A lens hood protects the front of the lens nearly as well and improves contrast at the same time. Using filters Match the thread size. The diameter is printed on the front of the lens, for example 67 mm. Step-up rings let one set of filters fit several lenses. Check the colour. Strong ND filters can add a slight colour cast. Shoot raw and fix it with the white balance picker."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Filters","u":"filters.html#exercises","x":"N1 Reflections Photograph a pond or a shop window while slowly turning a polariser. Take one picture at the weakest setting and one at the strongest. N2 Blue sky On a clear day, photograph the sky in four directions with a polariser and see where the effect is strongest. N3 Silky water With an ND filter and a tripod, photograph moving water at 1/250 s, 1/4 s, 2 s and 30 s. Compare the textures. N4 No filter needed Shoot a sunset as a bracket of exposures and blend them in editing. Compare the result with a single exposure."},{"t":"Focal length","c":"Focal length","u":"focal-length.html","x":"What your lens sees, and how it changes the picture. The focal length of a lens, measured in millimetres, describes how much of the scene it takes in and how big distant things look. A short focal length gives a wide view. A long one gives a narrow, magnified view. Zoom lenses cover a range, such as 24–70 mm. Prime lenses have a single focal length, like 50 mm, and they’re usually smaller and brighter for the money."},{"t":"Same spot, different lens","c":"Focal length","u":"focal-length.html#same-spot-different-lens","x":"Seven focal lengths from one spot. The whole picture is the 16 mm view, and each coloured frame shows a longer lens. Every view in the picture of the bay was taken from the same spot. Each coloured frame shows what a longer lens would capture. At 24 mm you see most of the bay, and at 200 mm the lighthouse fills the frame. The angle of view shrinks as the focal length grows, from 107° at 16 mm down to just 12° at 200 mm, measured across the diagonal of a full-frame sensor. A phone’s main camera sees roughly what a 24–26 mm lens does."},{"t":"Distance changes the background","c":"Focal length","u":"focal-length.html#distance-changes-the-background","x":"24 mm, standing close. Lots of background, and the lighthouse looks small and far away. 50 mm, a few steps back. Close to how your eyes see the scene. 135 mm, from far back. The lighthouse looms behind, large and soft. People often say wide-angle lenses distort and telephoto lenses compress. Strictly speaking, perspective depends only on where you stand. But you stand in different places with different lenses, so the effect is very real. To fill the frame with a person on a wide lens you have to get close, and then the background looks small and far away. With a long lens you step back for the same framing, and the background looks much bigger and closer behind them. That’s the classic compressed look. A long lens also shows a narrower slice of background, which is easier to keep simple, and at the same aperture it blurs that background more. That’s why lenses from 85 to 135 mm are so popular for portraits. From a comfortable distance they give faces natural proportions and a soft, uncluttered background. A wide lens close to a face makes the nose bigger and the ears smaller, which is rarely flattering."},{"t":"Lens families","c":"Focal length","u":"focal-length.html#lens-families","x":"Ultra-wide 10–20 mm A huge angle of view for big skies and tight interiors. Get close to something in the foreground or the picture will look empty. Keep the camera level so buildings don’t lean inwards. Wide 24–35 mm The classic range for landscapes and street photography, and the natural view of most phone cameras. Wide enough to show context, close enough to stay natural. Standard 40–60 mm Roughly matches the way we take in a scene, so pictures look natural and honest. A fast 50 mm prime at f/1.8 is small and sharp, and it doesn’t cost much. It makes an excellent first extra lens. Portrait 85–135 mm Flattering perspective for faces and a soft, simple background. Also good for picking out details in a landscape. Telephoto 200 mm and up Brings distant subjects close, like wildlife or sport. Every wobble is magnified too, so use a fast shutter speed or a tripod."},{"t":"Maximum aperture","c":"Focal length","u":"focal-length.html#maximum-aperture","x":"Every lens has a widest aperture, printed on the barrel as a number such as f/1.8 or f/2.8. Lenses with a wide maximum aperture are called fast, because they let you use faster shutter speeds in low light. They also give shallower depth of field. Many zooms have a maximum aperture that changes as you zoom, for example f/3.5 at the wide end and f/5.6 at the long end. Fast primes and constant-aperture zooms cost more, but they make a real difference indoors and at night."},{"t":"Zooms and primes","c":"Focal length","u":"focal-length.html#zooms-and-primes","x":"A zoom gives you lots of framings without changing lenses, which is handy when you’re travelling or can’t get closer. A prime has one focal length, and that limit is part of its charm. With a prime you walk more and look harder. Many photographers carry one standard zoom and one fast prime, and that pair covers a surprising amount of ground."},{"t":"Smaller sensors","c":"Focal length","u":"focal-length.html#smaller-sensors","x":"The focal lengths in this book are for full-frame cameras, whose sensor measures 36 × 24 mm. A smaller sensor only sees the middle of the image the lens projects, so every lens behaves like a longer one. To compare, multiply by the crop factor. That’s about 1.5 or 1.6 for APS-C sensors and 2 for Micro Four Thirds. A 35 mm lens on an APS-C camera frames much like a 50 mm lens on full frame. Phones usually quote their equivalent focal lengths directly. Zoom with your feet, then with your lens Choose the look, then the lens. Decide whether you want a wide, involving view or a compressed, isolated one. Then work out where to stand. Try one focal length for a week. Shooting with a single prime, or a zoom left at one setting, trains your eye faster than anything else."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Focal length","u":"focal-length.html#exercises","x":"F1 Same spot Stand in one place and photograph the same view at every focal length you have, widest to longest. Lay the pictures side by side. F2 Same framing Photograph a friend’s head and shoulders at your widest focal length from close up. Then do it at your longest from far back, keeping them the same size. Compare the background and the face. F3 One lens for a week Use a single focal length for a whole week, or leave your zoom at 35 or 50 mm. Move your feet instead of zooming. F4 Your phone in camera terms Photograph a scene with your phone’s main camera, then try to match the framing with your zoom. Note the focal length that matches. Now you know your phone’s lens in camera terms."},{"t":"Lens character","c":"Lens character","u":"lens-character.html","x":"Distortion, vignetting, colour fringes and flare, and what to do about them. No lens is perfect. Every design trades one compromise against another, and each lens leaves its own fingerprints on the picture. Some of them are faults you’ll want to correct in editing. Others, like a gentle darkening of the corners or a warm haze of flare, can become part of your style."},{"t":"Barrel and pincushion distortion","c":"Lens character","u":"lens-character.html#barrel-and-pincushion-distortion","x":"No distortion. Straight lines stay straight. Barrel. Lines bow outwards, typical of wide lenses. Pincushion. Lines pinch inwards, typical of long zooms. Moustache. A wavy mix of both. Distortion bends straight lines, most visibly near the edges of the frame. Barrel distortion bows them outwards, as if the picture were printed on the side of a barrel. It’s typical of wide-angle lenses and the wide end of zooms. Pincushion distortion pinches lines inwards towards the centre and shows up at the long end of many zooms. Some lenses mix the two, bowing lines out near the centre and in near the corners, a pattern often called moustache distortion. You notice distortion most with straight lines near the edges, like horizons and door frames. In a landscape without straight lines it rarely matters. Editing programs correct it automatically with a lens profile, a description of each lens’s particular faults. Turning profile corrections on is one of the simplest improvements you can make to pictures of buildings."},{"t":"Perspective distortion","c":"Lens character","u":"lens-character.html#perspective-distortion","x":"16 mm from close up · stretched edges 50 mm from further back · natural shapes Wide-angle lenses stretch things near the edges and corners of the frame. That’s not a fault in the lens. It’s what happens when a very wide view of the world is flattened onto a rectangle. Round things near the corners turn oval, and the people at the ends of a group photo look wider than those in the middle. The other side of wide-angle perspective is exaggerated size. Whatever is closest to the lens looks huge compared with everything behind it. That can be a powerful tool, making a flower tower over a valley. On a face it isn’t flattering. Keep people away from the corners of a wide frame, and for portraits step back and zoom in."},{"t":"Converging verticals","c":"Lens character","u":"lens-character.html#converging-verticals","x":"Camera tilted up · the sides lean in Camera level, or corrected · upright lines Tilt the camera up at a tall building and its sides lean towards each other, as if the building were falling backwards. This is perspective too, because the sensor is no longer parallel to the front of the building. Keeping the camera level and stepping back keeps the lines upright, though you’ll get more foreground in the picture. Editing programs can straighten converging verticals afterwards, using guides you line up with the edges of the building. Leave some room around the building when you shoot, because the correction crops the edges of the picture. Architectural photographers use shift lenses, which slide the lens upwards while the camera stays level, so the lines are straight from the start."},{"t":"Vignetting","c":"Lens character","u":"lens-character.html#vignetting","x":"Widest aperture · darker corners Two stops down · even brightness Vignetting is a darkening towards the corners of the frame. Almost every lens shows some at its widest aperture, and it fades as you stop down by a stop or two. Lens profiles remove it in editing. Many photographers add a little back on purpose, because darker corners pull the eye towards the middle of the picture."},{"t":"Chromatic aberration","c":"Lens character","u":"lens-character.html#chromatic-aberration","x":"Colour fringes along high-contrast edges After correction in editing Glass bends different colours of light by slightly different amounts. When a lens doesn’t bring them all to the same point, you see coloured fringes along high-contrast edges, like dark branches against a bright sky. One kind shows as red and blue edges towards the corners of the frame and is easy to remove in editing. The other shows as purple and green fringes around the point of focus, mostly at wide apertures. Stopping down helps, and editing programs have a defringe tool for whatever is left."},{"t":"Flare and ghosting","c":"Lens character","u":"lens-character.html#flare-and-ghosting","x":"Sun just outside the frame, no hood The same scene with a lens hood When a bright light shines into the lens, some of it bounces around between the glass elements. It can show up as a haze that washes out contrast, called veiling flare. It can also appear as a chain of coloured shapes across the frame, called ghosting, often shaped like the aperture. A lens hood blocks light coming from outside the frame and fixes most cases. When the sun is in the picture, try moving slightly so it hides behind a tree or the edge of a building. At small apertures like f/16 the sun turns into a star with sharp points, which many photographers use on purpose. Sometimes a warm haze of flare is exactly the mood you want."},{"t":"Sharpness across the frame","c":"Lens character","u":"lens-character.html#sharpness-across-the-frame","x":"Most lenses are sharpest in the centre and a little softer towards the corners, especially at their widest aperture. Stopping down two or three stops usually evens it out. Some lenses also focus on a slightly curved surface instead of a flat one, so the corners can look soft while the centre is sharp. For flat subjects like documents or paintings, a smaller aperture keeps the whole frame crisp. Lens character in practice Turn on profile corrections. They remove distortion and vignetting in one click. Use the hood. It costs nothing and improves contrast in almost every picture. Stop down for even sharpness. f/5.6 to f/8 is the sweet spot for most lenses. Keep people central with wide lenses. The edges and corners stretch faces and bodies."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Lens character","u":"lens-character.html#exercises","x":"C1 Straight lines Photograph a tiled wall or a door frame at your widest focal length, with the lines near the edges. Then apply the lens profile in editing and compare. C2 Leaning building Photograph a tall building with the camera tilted up, then from further back with the camera level. Straighten the first one in editing and compare the results. C3 Corners Photograph an evenly lit wall or a clear sky at your widest aperture and again at f/8. Compare the corners. C4 Flare test Shoot towards a bright light that sits just outside the frame, first without a hood and then with one, or with your hand shading the lens."},{"t":"Editing basics","c":"Editing basics","u":"editing-basics.html","x":"A simple order of steps from raw file to finished picture. Editing isn’t cheating. Every digital photo gets processed, either by the camera’s own software or by you. Doing it yourself, from a raw file, gives you the most control. The order of steps below works in nearly every editing program, and following it saves you from undoing earlier work."},{"t":"Raw or JPEG","c":"Editing basics","u":"editing-basics.html#raw-or-jpeg","x":"A JPEG is a finished picture. The camera has already set its white balance, contrast, sharpening and colour, and thrown away the data it didn’t need. A raw file keeps everything the sensor recorded. That gives you far more room to rescue highlights or change the white balance without losing quality. Raw files are bigger and always need processing, which is why many photographers record both."},{"t":"A good order of work","c":"Editing basics","u":"editing-basics.html#a-good-order-of-work","x":"Crop and straighten. Fix the horizon and the framing first, because every later step depends on what’s left in the picture. Set the white balance. Click a neutral spot with the picker, or move temperature and tint until whites look white. Set the exposure. Adjust the overall brightness while you watch the histogram. Shape the tones. Bring back detail with the highlights and shadows sliders, then set the end points with whites and blacks. Add contrast and colour. Small amounts go a long way. Push saturation too far and skin starts to look orange. Make local adjustments. Brighten a face or darken a bright corner with a brush or a mask. Remove distractions. Clone away dust spots and small bright things near the edges. Sharpen and reduce noise. Do this last, and judge it at 100% on screen."},{"t":"Masks and local adjustments","c":"Editing basics","u":"editing-basics.html#masks-and-local-adjustments","x":"Global sliders change the whole picture. Masks let you change just one part of it. Most programs can now select the sky or a person automatically, and you can paint masks by hand with a brush. A typical use is darkening a bright sky a little while brightening the foreground, which does what a graduated filter does in the field. Keep local changes subtle. If people notice the edit before the picture, it’s too strong."},{"t":"Black and white","c":"Editing basics","u":"editing-basics.html#black-and-white","x":"The colour original Black and white, with blue darkened Converting to black and white is more than removing colour. Each colour turns into a shade of grey, and a good conversion lets you decide how bright each one becomes. Darkening blue makes skies dramatic, as a red filter did in the film days. Brightening orange and red lightens skin. Pictures with strong shapes and contrast tend to work best without colour."},{"t":"Exporting for screen and print","c":"Editing basics","u":"editing-basics.html#exporting-for-screen-and-print","x":"For sharing online, export a JPEG in the sRGB colour space. Around 2,000 to 2,500 pixels on the long side is plenty for most screens and keeps files small. If a website or a competition asks for a particular size, use that. For print, the usual guide is 300 pixels per inch at the final size. A 20 × 30 cm print needs about 2,400 × 3,500 pixels. Most labs accept sRGB JPEGs. Screens are much brighter than paper, so prints often come out darker than you expect. Brightening the print version a little, or calibrating your screen, helps a lot."},{"t":"Keeping your photos safe","c":"Editing basics","u":"editing-basics.html#keeping-your-photos-safe","x":"Every hard drive fails one day. The usual advice is the 3-2-1 rule, three copies of every photo on two different kinds of storage, with one copy somewhere else, like a cloud service or a drive at a friend’s house. Back up after every shoot, before you format the memory card. Editing habits Compare before and after. Your eyes get used to strong edits quickly, so switch back to the original often. Sleep on it. Look at an edit again the next day before you share it. Use presets as a start. Save your favourite settings, then adjust each picture on its own. Rate and tag. Star ratings and keywords make it easy to find the best pictures years later."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Editing basics","u":"editing-basics.html#exercises","x":"P1 One picture, every step Take one raw file through every step in the order from chapter 12. Save a version after each step and compare the first with the last. P2 Black and white Convert a colour landscape to black and white. Adjust the blue and orange channels until the sky and the land separate well. P3 Local adjustment Darken a bright sky with a mask while brightening the foreground. Keep it subtle enough that a friend can’t spot the edit. P4 Print test Print one photo small and hold it next to your screen. Adjust the brightness of the print version until the two match."},{"t":"Talking about photographs","c":"Talking about photographs","u":"talking-about-photographs.html","x":"How to give useful feedback at a club evening, and how to take it. Seeing other people’s pictures, and hearing what they see in yours, is one of the best reasons to join a club. Good feedback helps a photographer see their own work with fresh eyes. Careless feedback just makes people stop sharing. A few habits make the difference."},{"t":"Look first, talk later","c":"Talking about photographs","u":"talking-about-photographs.html#look-first-talk-later","x":"Give a photo some time before you say anything. Notice where your eye goes first and where it travels after that. Ask yourself what the photographer wanted to show, and what you feel when you look at it. Your first reaction is useful information, as long as you treat it as your reaction and not as a fact about the picture."},{"t":"Describe before you suggest","c":"Talking about photographs","u":"talking-about-photographs.html#describe-before-you-suggest","x":"Start by describing what you see and what it does for you. Only then suggest changes. “The light catches the rim of the cup and my eye goes straight there” is far more useful than “nice light”. Name specific things, like the height of the horizon or the colour of a shadow, so the photographer has something to work with. Phrase suggestions as possibilities. “I wonder how it would look with a little more space above the head” leaves room for what the photographer intended. They may have had a reason you can’t see."},{"t":"Use a shared vocabulary","c":"Talking about photographs","u":"talking-about-photographs.html#use-a-shared-vocabulary","x":"The chapters on composition and light give the club a common language. Saying a picture has strong side light, or that a leading line runs out of the frame, is clearer than saying it doesn’t quite work. It also points the photographer to the right part of this handbook."},{"t":"Receiving feedback","c":"Talking about photographs","u":"talking-about-photographs.html#receiving-feedback","x":"Listen first and save your explanations for afterwards. If people misread a picture, that tells you something about what the picture says on its own, because you won’t be standing next to it when others see it. Thank people and take notes. Decide later what to act on. You don’t have to agree with every comment, and some of the best pictures ignore the advice of the room."},{"t":"Running a critique evening","c":"Talking about photographs","u":"talking-about-photographs.html#running-a-critique-evening","x":"Small groups of four to six work better than one big circle, because everyone gets a chance to speak. Keep each picture to a few minutes. Let the photographer say one sentence about it first, or nothing at all if they want an unbiased reaction. Changing who speaks first each round stops the most confident voices from setting the tone every time. Feedback that helps Be specific. Point at the part of the picture you mean. Start with what works. Strengths are as useful to know as weaknesses. Suggest, don’t prescribe. Offer ideas the photographer can try out. Talk about the picture. Comment on the work, never on the person."},{"t":"Exercises","c":"Talking about photographs","u":"talking-about-photographs.html#exercises","x":"R1 A silent minute Look at a photo for a full minute before you speak. Write down where your eye goes first and where it goes next. R2 Describe, then suggest Give feedback on a few pictures using the order in chapter 13. Ask the photographers which comments helped them most. R3 Your own picture Pick one of your photos and write a short critique of it as if someone else had taken it. R4 Ask the room At the next club evening, ask one specific question about your picture instead of asking what people think of it."},{"t":"Aperture","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-aperture","x":"The adjustable opening inside the lens, written as an f-number. A smaller number means a wider opening."},{"t":"Autofocus (AF-S, AF-C)","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-autofocus","x":"The camera focusing by itself, either once and then locking, or continuously while you hold the button."},{"t":"Back light","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-back-light","x":"Light coming from behind the subject towards the camera. It gives glowing outlines and silhouettes."},{"t":"Barrel distortion","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-barrel-distortion","x":"Straight lines bowing outwards near the edges of the frame, typical of wide-angle lenses."},{"t":"Bokeh","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-bokeh","x":"The look of the out-of-focus parts of a picture, especially points of light in the background."},{"t":"Bracketing","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-bracketing","x":"Taking several shots of the same scene at different exposures, to choose from or to blend later."},{"t":"Chromatic aberration","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-chromatic-aberration","x":"Coloured fringes along high-contrast edges, caused by a lens bending colours by different amounts."},{"t":"Clipping","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-clipping","x":"Areas recorded as pure black or pure white, with no detail left to recover."},{"t":"Colour temperature","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-colour-temperature","x":"The colour of light measured in kelvin. Low numbers are warm and orange, high numbers cool and blue."},{"t":"Converging verticals","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-converging-verticals","x":"Upright lines leaning towards each other when the camera tilts up or down."},{"t":"Crop factor","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-crop-factor","x":"How much a smaller sensor narrows the view compared with full frame, about 1.5 for APS-C."},{"t":"Depth of field","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-depth-of-field","x":"The zone from near to far that looks acceptably sharp in a photo."},{"t":"Diffraction","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-diffraction","x":"A slight overall softness at very small apertures such as f/22."},{"t":"Exposure compensation","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-exposure-compensation","x":"Telling the camera to make the picture brighter or darker than its meter suggests, in steps of a stop."},{"t":"Exposure value (EV)","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-exposure-value","x":"A single number for a combination of settings. One EV equals one stop."},{"t":"Fill flash","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-fill-flash","x":"A little flash used in daylight to soften shadows."},{"t":"Flare","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-flare","x":"Stray light bouncing inside the lens, seen as haze or coloured shapes."},{"t":"Focal length","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-focal-length","x":"Measured in millimetres, it sets how wide or narrow the view of a lens is."},{"t":"Focus peaking","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-focus-peaking","x":"A display aid that outlines sharp edges in colour while you focus by hand."},{"t":"Golden hour","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-golden-hour","x":"The hour after sunrise and the hour before sunset, when light is low, warm and soft."},{"t":"Hard light","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-hard-light","x":"Light from a small source, giving crisp shadows and high contrast."},{"t":"Histogram","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-histogram","x":"A graph of how many pixels have each brightness, from black on the left to white on the right."},{"t":"Hyperfocal distance","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-hyperfocal-distance","x":"The closest focus distance that still keeps the far distance acceptably sharp."},{"t":"ISO","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-iso","x":"How much the sensor’s signal is amplified. Higher ISO brightens the picture and adds noise."},{"t":"Kelvin","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-kelvin","x":"The unit of colour temperature, written K."},{"t":"Lens profile","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-lens-profile","x":"Data an editing program uses to correct a particular lens’s distortion and vignetting."},{"t":"Metering mode","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-metering-mode","x":"Which part of the frame the camera’s light meter pays attention to."},{"t":"Middle grey","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-middle-grey","x":"The tone a light meter aims for, a grey that reflects 18% of the light."},{"t":"ND filter","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-nd-filter","x":"Neutral density filter. Dark grey glass that cuts light without changing colours."},{"t":"Negative space","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-negative-space","x":"Empty area around a subject, used on purpose."},{"t":"Noise","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-noise","x":"Grainy speckles and blotches of colour, most visible at high ISO and in dark areas."},{"t":"Pincushion distortion","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-pincushion-distortion","x":"Straight lines bending inwards near the edges, typical of long zooms."},{"t":"Polariser","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-polariser","x":"A filter that cuts reflections and darkens blue skies when rotated."},{"t":"Prime lens","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-prime-lens","x":"A lens with a single focal length."},{"t":"Raw file","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-raw-file","x":"The unprocessed data from the sensor, with the most room for editing."},{"t":"Shutter speed","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-shutter-speed","x":"How long the sensor collects light, written in seconds or fractions of a second."},{"t":"Side light","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-side-light","x":"Light from one side of the subject, which shows shape and texture."},{"t":"Soft light","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-soft-light","x":"Light from a large source, giving gentle shadows and low contrast."},{"t":"Stop","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-stop","x":"A doubling or halving of light."},{"t":"Vignetting","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-vignetting","x":"Darkening towards the corners of the frame."},{"t":"White balance","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-white-balance","x":"The camera setting that makes white things look white in a given light."},{"t":"Zoom lens","c":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html#g-zoom-lens","x":"A lens whose focal length can be changed, such as 24–70 mm."}];
